# +Jobs, +Workers, +Budget, -Term
# +Jobs, +Workers, +Budget, -Times
# +Jobs, +Workers, -Least
# ?Point, ?Count, ?Workers, ?Seed, ?Slack, ?Kind
#   * <module> The least time to finish all jobs
#
# THE PROBLEM (LeetCode 1723, "Find Minimum Time to Finish All Jobs"): jobs
# of given lengths and W workers; each job goes to one worker, and a
# worker's time is the sum of its jobs. What is the least possible largest
# time? Asked as the set of the largest times at most T of the assignments;
# T is the least, so the set is that one number.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM, in continuation passing style, the times left to each worker
# a Scott list of unary numerals, with the prelude's natural_at_least,
# natural_difference and foldr (the API of round-37-directions.md), the
# least of the times left taken by foldr of the program's own min:
#
#     assign  = Y (\self. \caps. \j. \f.
#                 caps {} (\c. \others.
#                   { at_least c j (f (cons (difference c j) others)) {},
#                     self others j (\rest. f (cons c rest)) }))
#     place   = Y (\self. \jobs. \caps. \k.
#                 jobs (k caps) (\j. \rest. assign caps j (\next. self rest next k)))
#     min     = \a. \b. at_least a b b a
#     program = place Jobs [T, ..., T] (\caps. difference T (foldr min T caps))
#
# Directions: A (the assignments are W^n, the native search is over the
# subsets of the jobs, one worker at a time, as in Held and Karp's
# programme over subsets); B (merging by value: many assignments leave the
# same times, the state is the rest of the jobs and the list of the times
# left, and the times are spelled by nested differences); D (foldr applied
# to a function of the program, natural_at_least and natural_difference
# called directly and inside it); G (the unary arithmetic of the times).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. S(P) is the set of the largest times at most T of the
# assignments of the jobs to the W workers in which every worker's time is
# at most T: finishing_times/4 on small instances. Every one is at least the
# least largest time, least_finishing_time/3, so for T the least S(P) is
# {T}, and for T one less it is empty.
#
# THE LEMMAS, on the definitions of the library functions (contest_attractors
# attractor_source/2 and the prelude), not on their native references.
# ⌜n⌝ is the Scott unary numeral, ⌜0⌝ = λz.λs. z and ⌜n + 1⌝ = λz.λs. s ⌜n⌝,
# which unary/2 builds exactly; true = λt.λf. t and false = λt.λf. f; a list
# is a Scott list, cons h t one β step from λn.λc. c h t.
# - (Y) fixpoint G =β G (fixpoint G), two β steps.
# - (AtLeast) at_least = Y (λs.λm.λn. n true (λn1. m false (λm1. s m1 n1))).
#   By induction on n, at_least ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β true when m >= n and false
#   otherwise.
# - (Difference) difference = Y (λs.λm.λn. n m (λn1. m ⌜0⌝ (λm1. s m1 n1))).
#   By induction on n, difference ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β ⌜m - n⌝ when m >= n.
# - (Min) min ⌜a⌝ ⌜b⌝ →β at_least ⌜a⌝ ⌜b⌝ ⌜b⌝ ⌜a⌝ =β ⌜min(a, b)⌝ (AtLeast).
# - (Foldr) foldr = Y (λs.λf.λz.λxs. xs z (λh.λt. f h (s f z t))). By
#   induction on the list, foldr min ⌜T⌝ [⌜c1⌝, ..., ⌜cW⌝] =β
#   ⌜min(T, c1, ..., cW)⌝ (Min).
# - (Assign) assign [⌜c1⌝, ..., ⌜cW⌝] ⌜j⌝ f is β-equal to a nest of sets
#   {A1, {A2, ... {AW, {}}}}, Ai β-equal to f applied to the list with ci
#   replaced by ⌜ci - j⌝ when ci >= j, and to {} when ci < j (AtLeast,
#   Difference), by induction on W: the second member of each level is
#   assign on the rest, its continuation putting ⌜ci⌝ back in front.
# - (Empty) If U →* {} by β and η steps, no pure term is reached from U by
#   →c steps. Every →c step keeps U →* {}: a β or η step by confluence, {}
#   being normal; a member replacement of a set s by Lemma 2a of
#   certificate_meta_lemmas.md, which replays U →* {} to {} with the
#   descendants of s replaced, and {} is no descendant of s, s having a
#   member and descendants keeping the number of members. A pure term
#   reduces only to pure terms, and {} is not pure.
#
# So place [j1, ..., jn] caps k, with caps =β the list of the numerals of
# the times left, is β-equal to k caps when there is no job, and otherwise
# to the nest of Assign for j1, whose member for a worker with time left
# c >= j1 is β-equal to place [j2, ..., jn] caps' k, caps' with c replaced
# by c - j1.
#
# PROOF, with ⟦·⟧, D, S and Th and Lemma 2b of
# gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For an assignment whose every worker's time is at most T, give the
#   jobs in order to their workers: at each job, the worker's time left is
#   T less the jobs given to it so far, at least the job's length; take its
#   member of the nest of Assign, by Lemma 2b.1 on the β-equal term. After
#   the last job the term is β-equal to k caps, caps the list of the
#   numerals T - t1, ..., T - tW, ti the workers' times, and so to
#   difference ⌜T⌝ ⌜min(T, T - t1, ..., T - tW)⌝ =β ⌜max(t1, ..., tW)⌝
#   (Foldr, Difference), pure and normal: it is in that term's meaning
#   (equal terms, Lemma 2b.2) and so in ⟦P⟧ (Lemma 2b.2).
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term P reaches by a finite
#   sequence σ. Every set of P and of its reducts is one written in assign,
#   or {}; each chosen branch is the whole term, every call of place and of
#   assign's continuation being in tail position. The terms β-equal to {}
#   (a worker whose time left is shorter than the job, the end of the
#   workers) reach no pure term (Empty). So a pure term is reached only
#   through an assignment of every job to a worker whose time left covers
#   it, that is an assignment with every time at most T, and the term is
#   then β-equal to ⌜max(t1, ..., tW)⌝, which is at most T.
#
# THE SUBTASKS AND THE TRAPS: workers_point/6 below. The traps: T one less
# than the least (empty), and a near miss, the jobs of a subtask with one job
# lengthened by one so that the least largest time grows past T (empty).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHM: the dynamic programme over the subsets of the jobs, the
# subsets one worker can finish within T found first (M. Held, R. M. Karp,
# A Dynamic Programming Approach to Sequencing Problems, J. SIAM 1962,
# doi:10.1137/0110015); tabled evaluation keyed by the value of the rest of
# the jobs and of the times left (W. Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996,
# doi:10.1145/227595.227597).

